#{[17592186295167 :question/worked-solution "The volume of a parallelepiped spanned by three vectors is given by the absolute value of their triple product.\n\nFirst, we compute the triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (y\\times z) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 6 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 6 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(2\\cdot 1-5\\cdot 0)-(0\\cdot 1-5\\cdot 6)+4(0\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 6) \\\\  & =6+30-48 \\\\  & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the absolute value. So, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped is \n\n$$\nV=|-12|=12.\n$$"] [17592186295168 :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (y\\times z) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 & k \\\\ 1 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix}-0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{vmatrix}+k\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(4\\cdot 5-3\\cdot 2)+0+k(1\\cdot 2-4\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =2(20-6)+2k \\\\  & =28+2k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (y\\times z) & =0 \\\\ 28+2k & =0 \\\\ 2k & =-28 \\\\ k & =-14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=-14.$"] [17592186295169 :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\na\\cdot (b\\times c)=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & k & 5\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ k & 5\\end{vmatrix}-3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix}+0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & k\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(4\\cdot 5-2\\cdot k)-3(0\\cdot 5-2\\cdot 2)=(20-2k)+12 \\\\  & =32-2k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =0 \\\\ 32-2k & =0 \\\\ -2k & =-32 \\\\ k & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=16.$"] [17592186295170 :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & k & k \\\\ 0 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{vmatrix}-k\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{vmatrix}+k\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(3\\cdot 5-2\\cdot 0)-k(0\\cdot 5-2\\cdot 4)+k(0\\cdot 0-3\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =15+8k-12k \\\\  & =15-4k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =0 \\\\ 15-4k & =0 \\\\ 4k & =15 \\\\ k & =\\frac{15}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=\\frac{15}{4}.$"] [17592186295171 :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & {k}^{2} & 2 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}5 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-{k}^{2}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4(5\\cdot 0-3\\cdot 1)-{k}^{2}(1\\cdot 0-3\\cdot 2)+2(1\\cdot 1-5\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =4(-3)-{k}^{2}(0-6)+2(1-10) \\\\  & =-12+6{k}^{2}-18 \\\\  & =6{k}^{2}-30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSet the triple product equal to 0:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{k}^{2}-30 & =0 \\\\ 6{k}^{2} & =30 \\\\ {k}^{2} & =5 \\\\ k & =\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if \n$k=-\\sqrt{5}$ or $k=\\sqrt{5}.$"] [17592186295173 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(3i+4j+5k)-(i+2k) \\\\  & =2i+4j+3k \\\\  & =⟨2,4,3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(6i+2j+4k)-(i+2k) \\\\  & =5i+2j+2k \\\\  & =⟨5,2,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =d-a \\\\  & =(5i+3j+6k)-(i+2k) \\\\  & =4i+3j+4k \\\\  & =⟨4,3,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix}-4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{vmatrix}+3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(2\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 3)-4(5\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 4)+3(5\\cdot 3-2\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =2(8-6)-4(20-8)+3(15-8) \\\\  & =4-48+21 \\\\  & =-23.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|-23|=23.\n$$"] [17592186295174 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(5i+1j+3k)-(3i+2k) \\\\  & =2i+j+k \\\\  & =⟨2,1,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(4i+3j+5k)-(3i+2k) \\\\  & =i+3j+3k \\\\  & =⟨1,3,3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =d-a \\\\  & =(3i+5k)-(3i+2k) \\\\  & =3k \\\\  & =⟨0,0,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}-1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(3\\cdot 3-3\\cdot 0)-1(1\\cdot 3-3\\cdot 0)+1(1\\cdot 0-3\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =2(9)-1(3)+1(0) \\\\  & =18-3+0 \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|15|=15.\n$$"] [17592186295175 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(5j+5k)-(1j+3k) \\\\  & =4j+2k \\\\  & =⟨0,4,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(3i+4j+5k)-(1j+3k) \\\\  & =3i+3j+2k \\\\  & =⟨3,3,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =d-a \\\\  & =(2i+2k)-(1j+3k) \\\\  & =2i-1j-1k \\\\  & =⟨2,-1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}-4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}+2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-4(3\\cdot -1-2\\cdot 2)+2(3\\cdot -1-3\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =28-18 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|10|=10.\n$$"] [17592186295176 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(2i-3j+4k)-(-i+1k) \\\\  & =3i-3j+3k \\\\  & =⟨3,-3,3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(-2i-2j+2k)-(-i+1k) \\\\  & =-i-2j+1k \\\\  & =⟨-1,-2,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =d-a \\\\  & =(-j+5k)-(-i+1k) \\\\  & =i-j+4k \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -3 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{vmatrix}-(-3)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{vmatrix}+3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(-8+1)+3(-4-1)+3(1-(-2)) \\\\  & =-21-15+9 \\\\  & =-27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|-27|=27.\n$$"] [17592186295221 :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\times v & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -5 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(4\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot (-5))i-(3\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 2)j+(3\\cdot (-5)-4\\cdot 2)k \\\\  & =(8-5)i-(6+2)j+(-15-8)k \\\\  & =3i-8j-23k \\\\  & =⟨3,-8,-23⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186295222 :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\times v & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(2\\cdot -1-0\\cdot 0)i-(\\sqrt{3}\\cdot -1-0\\cdot 1)j+(\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 1)k \\\\  & =-2i+\\sqrt{3}j-2k \\\\  & =⟨-2,\\sqrt{3},-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186295223 :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\times v & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((0)(-3)-(1)(2))i-(\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)(-3)-(1)(1))j+(\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)(2)-(0)(1))k \\\\  & =(0-2)i-(-2-1)j+\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)k \\\\  & =-2i+3j+\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)k \\\\  & =⟨-2,3,\\frac{4}{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186295227 :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & -4 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 6\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-4)(6)-(1)(5))i-((3)(6)-(1)(-2))j+((3)(5)-(-4)(-2))k \\\\  & =(-24-5)i-(18+2)j+(15-8)k \\\\  & =-29\\,i-20\\,j+7\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186295228 :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 2 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 1-(-5)(4))i-(1\\cdot 1-(-5)(-3))j+(1\\cdot 4-2\\cdot (-3))k \\\\  & =(2+20)i-(1-15)j+(4+6)k \\\\  & =22\\,i+14\\,j+10\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186295229 :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -6 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-6 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-6 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 5-2\\cdot -1)i-(-6\\cdot 5-2\\cdot 4)j+(-6\\cdot -1-3\\cdot 4)k \\\\  & =(15+2)i-(-30-8)j+(6-12)k \\\\  & =17\\,i+38\\,j-6\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186295230 :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -7 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-7 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -6\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -6\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -7 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-7)(-6)-(4)(3))i-((2)(-6)-(4)(5))j+((2)(3)-(-7)(5))k \\\\  & =(42-12)i-(-12-20)j+(6+35)k \\\\  & =30\\,i+32\\,j+41\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186295231 :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -4 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(0)(-2)-(3)(2)i-(-4)(-2)-(3)(1)j+(-4)(2)-(0)(1)k \\\\  & =(0-6)i-(8-3)j+(-8-0)k \\\\  & =-6\\,i-5\\,j-8\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186295235 :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2x & 3 & 1 \\\\ -2x & 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}2x & 1 \\\\ -2x & 4\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}2x & 3 \\\\ -2x & 0\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(12-0)i-(8x+2x)j+(0+6x)k \\\\  & =⟨12,-10x,6x⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{{12}^{2}+(-10x)^{2}+(6x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{144+100{x}^{2}+36{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{136{x}^{2}+144}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{280} \\\\ \\sqrt{136{x}^{2}+144} & =\\sqrt{280} \\\\ 136{x}^{2}+144 & =280 \\\\ 136{x}^{2} & =136 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-1,$ $x=1.$"] [17592186295236 :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ x & 0 & 1 \\\\ -x & 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}x & 1 \\\\ -x & 0\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}x & 0 \\\\ -x & 2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(0-2)i-(0+x)j+(2x+0)k \\\\  & =⟨-2,-x,2x⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-x)^{2}+(2x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+{x}^{2}+4{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{x}^{2}+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{29} \\\\ \\sqrt{5{x}^{2}+4} & =\\sqrt{29} \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+4 & =29 \\\\ 5{x}^{2} & =25 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-\\sqrt{5},$ $x=\\sqrt{5}.$"] [17592186295237 :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ x & 3 & 0 \\\\ -x & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}x & 0 \\\\ -x & 1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}x & 3 \\\\ -x & 0\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(3-0)i-(x+0)j+(0+3x)k \\\\  & =⟨3,-x,3x⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-x)^{2}+(3x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+{x}^{2}+9{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10{x}^{2}+9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{49} \\\\ \\sqrt{10{x}^{2}+9} & =\\sqrt{49} \\\\ 10{x}^{2}+9 & =49 \\\\ 10{x}^{2} & =40 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-2,$ $x=2.$"] [17592186295238 :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ x & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}x & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}x & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(3-0)i-(3x-1)j+(0-1)k \\\\  & =⟨3,1-3x,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(1-3x)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+(9{x}^{2}-6x+1)+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{x}^{2}-6x+11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{10} \\\\ \\sqrt{9{x}^{2}-6x+11} & =\\sqrt{10} \\\\ 9{x}^{2}-6x+11 & =10 \\\\ (3x-1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ 3x-1 & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=\\frac{1}{3}.$"] [17592186295239 :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ x & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}x & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}x & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(2-0)i-(2x-2)j+(0-1)k \\\\  & =⟨2,2-2x,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(2-2x)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+(4{x}^{2}-8x+4)+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-8x+9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-8x+9} & =\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-8x+9 & =5 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-8x+4 & =0 \\\\ (2x-2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2x-2 & =0 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=1.$"] [17592186295240 :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ x & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}x & 5 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}x & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(4-0)i-(x-5)j+(0-4)k \\\\  & =⟨4,5-x,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+(5-x)^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+(25-10x+{x}^{2})+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-10x+57}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{33} \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-10x+57} & =\\sqrt{33} \\\\ {x}^{2}-10x+57 & =33 \\\\ {x}^{2}-10x+24 & =0 \\\\ (x-4)(x-6) & =0 \\\\ x & =4,\\,x=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=4,$ $x=6.$"] [17592186295241 :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2x & 1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}2x & 4 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}2x & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(2-0)i-(4x-12)j+(0-3)k \\\\  & =⟨2,12-4x,-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(12-4x)^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+(144-96x+16{x}^{2})+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{x}^{2}-96x+157}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{157} \\\\ \\sqrt{16{x}^{2}-96x+157} & =\\sqrt{157} \\\\ 16{x}^{2}-96x+157 & =157 \\\\ 16{x}^{2}-96x & =0 \\\\ 16x(x-6) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,x=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=0,$ $x=6.$"] [17592186297464 :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 5 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(5\\cdot 2-0\\cdot (-1))i-((-3)\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 4)j+((-3)\\cdot (-1)-5\\cdot 4)k \\\\  & =10i+6j-17k \\\\  & =⟨10,6,-17⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the corresponding parallelogram is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\,a\\times b\\,| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{10}^{2}+{6}^{2}+(-17)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{100+36+289} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{425} \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186297465 :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 4-0\\cdot 3)i-(1\\cdot 4-0\\cdot 0)j+(1\\cdot 3-0\\cdot 0)k \\\\  & =0i-4j+3k \\\\  & =⟨0,-4,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the corresponding parallelogram is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\,a\\times b\\,| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+16+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186297466 :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\times q & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 6 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 6 & 1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(5\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 0)i-(0\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 6)j+(0\\cdot 0-5\\cdot 6)k \\\\  & =5i-6j-30k \\\\  & =⟨5,-6,-30⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the corresponding parallelogram is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\,p\\times q\\,| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+(-6)^{2}+(-30)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25+36+900} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{961} \\\\  & =31.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of the triangle is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{31}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186297467 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =q-p \\\\  & =(3i+j-k)-(i-2j) \\\\  & =2i+3j-k \\\\  & =⟨2,3,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =r-p \\\\  & =(-i+2k)-(i-2j) \\\\  & =-2i+2j+2k \\\\  & =⟨-2,2,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\times \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 2)i-(2\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot (-2))j+(2\\cdot 2-3\\cdot (-2))k \\\\  & =8i-2j+10k \\\\  & =⟨8,-2,10⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\times \\overset{\\to }{PR}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{8}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{10}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{64+4+100} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{168} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{42}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,PQR$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{2\\sqrt{42}}{2}=\\sqrt{42}.\n$$"] [17592186297468 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{DE}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{DF}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DE} & =e-d \\\\  & =(-j)-(-2i+j+3k) \\\\  & =2i-2j-3k \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,-3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{DF} & =f-d \\\\  & =(2i+2j-k)-(-2i+j+3k) \\\\  & =4i+j-4k \\\\  & =⟨4,1,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DE}\\times \\overset{\\to }{DF} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -4\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot (-4)-(-3)\\cdot 1)i-(2\\cdot (-4)-(-3)\\cdot 4)j+(2\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot 4)k \\\\  & =11i-4j+10k \\\\  & =⟨11,-4,10⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{DE}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{DF}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{DE}\\times \\overset{\\to }{DF}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{11}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{10}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{121+16+100} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{237}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,DEF$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{237}}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186297469 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM} & =m-l \\\\  & =(2j-2k)-(-3i+k) \\\\  & =3i+2j-3k \\\\  & =⟨3,2,-3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =n-l \\\\  & =(2i-j)-(-3i+k) \\\\  & =5i-j-k \\\\  & =⟨5,-1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 5 & -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(2\\cdot (-1)-(-3)\\cdot (-1))i-(3\\cdot (-1)-(-3)\\cdot 5)j+(3\\cdot (-1)-2\\cdot 5)k \\\\  & =-5i-12j-13k \\\\  & =⟨-5,-12,-13⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-5)^{2}+(-12)^{2}+(-13)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25+144+169} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{338} \\\\  & =13\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,LMN$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{13\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186297473 :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $NP$ can be related to the area of $△\\,LMN$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,NP\\cdot LM.\n$$\n\nSo, to find $NP,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM} & =m-l \\\\  & =(2j+k)-(3i-j) \\\\  & =-3i+3j+k \\\\  & =⟨-3,3,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =n-l \\\\  & =(-2i+j-k)-(3i-j) \\\\  & =-5i+2j-k \\\\  & =⟨-5,2,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -5 & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 3 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(3\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot 2)i-((-3)\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot (-5))j+((-3)\\cdot 2-3\\cdot (-5))k \\\\  & =-5i-8j+9k \\\\  & =⟨-5,-8,9⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-5)^{2}+(-8)^{2}+{9}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{170}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,LMN$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{170}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $LM$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}LM & =|\\overset{\\to }{LM}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,NP\\cdot LM,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,NP\\cdot LM \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{170}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,NP\\cdot \\sqrt{19} \\\\ \\sqrt{170} & =NP\\cdot \\sqrt{19} \\\\ NP & =\\frac{\\sqrt{170}}{\\sqrt{19}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3230}}{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186297474 :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $UT$ can be related to the area of $△\\,UVW$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,UT\\cdot VW.\n$$\n\nSo, to find $UT,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{VW}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{VU}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{VW} & =w-v \\\\  & =(3i-k)-(i-j+2k) \\\\  & =2i+j-3k \\\\  & =⟨2,1,-3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{VU} & =u-v \\\\  & =(-2i+2j)-(i-j+2k) \\\\  & =-3i+3j-2k \\\\  & =⟨-3,3,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{VW}\\times \\overset{\\to }{VU} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(1\\cdot (-2)-(-3)\\cdot 3)i-(2\\cdot (-2)-(-3)\\cdot (-3))j+(2\\cdot 3-1\\cdot (-3))k \\\\  & =7i+13j+9k \\\\  & =⟨7,13,9⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{VW}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{VU}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{VW}\\times \\overset{\\to }{VU}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{7}^{2}+{13}^{2}+{9}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{299}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,UVW$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{299}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $VW$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}VW & =|\\overset{\\to }{VW}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,UT\\cdot VW,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,UT\\cdot VW \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{299}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,UT\\cdot \\sqrt{14} \\\\ \\sqrt{299} & =UT\\cdot \\sqrt{14} \\\\ UT & =\\frac{\\sqrt{299}}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{4186}}{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186297475 :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $VL$ can be related to the area of $△\\,TUV$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}VL\\cdot TU\n$$\n\nSo, to find $VL,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{TU}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{TV}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{TU} & =u-t \\\\  & =(3i+2j-k)-(i+k) \\\\  & =2i+2j-2k \\\\  & =⟨2,2,-2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{TV} & =v-t \\\\  & =(-i+j)-(i+k) \\\\  & =-2i+j-k \\\\  & =⟨-2,1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{TU}\\times \\overset{\\to }{TV} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(2\\cdot (-1)-(-2)\\cdot 1)i-(2\\cdot (-1)-(-2)\\cdot (-2))j+(2\\cdot 1-2\\cdot (-2))k \\\\  & =0i+6j+6k \\\\  & =⟨0,6,6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{TU}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{TV}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{TU}\\times \\overset{\\to }{TV}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{6}^{2}+{6}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{72} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,TUV$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{6\\sqrt{2}}{2}=3\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $TU$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TU & =|\\overset{\\to }{TU}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{12} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}VL\\cdot TU,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}VL\\cdot TU \\\\ 3\\sqrt{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}VL\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 3\\sqrt{2} & =VL\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\ VL & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ VL & =\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186297477 :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $IP$ can be related to the area of $△\\,HIJ$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}IP\\cdot HJ\n$$\n\nSo, to find $IP,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{HJ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{HI}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{HJ} & =j-h \\\\  & =(-2j+k)-(-i+2j) \\\\  & =i-4j+k \\\\  & =⟨1,-4,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{HI} & =i-h \\\\  & =(i+j+3k)-(-i+2j) \\\\  & =2i-j+3k \\\\  & =⟨2,-1,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{HJ}\\times \\overset{\\to }{HI} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -4 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-4)\\cdot 3-1\\cdot (-1))i-(1\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 2)j+(1\\cdot (-1)-(-4)\\cdot 2)k \\\\  & =-11i-j+7k \\\\  & =⟨-11,-1,7⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{HJ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{HI}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{HJ}\\times \\overset{\\to }{HI}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-11)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{7}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{171} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,HIJ$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{3\\sqrt{19}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $HJ$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}HJ & =|\\overset{\\to }{HJ}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}IP\\cdot HJ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}IP\\cdot HJ \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{19}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,IP\\cdot 3\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{19} & =\\sqrt{2}\\,IP \\\\ IP & =\\frac{\\sqrt{19}}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{38}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186297486 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{DE}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{DF}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DE} & =e-d \\\\  & =(2i+k)-(-i+2j) \\\\  & =3i-2j+k \\\\  & =⟨3,-2,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{DF} & =f-d \\\\  & =(i+3j+2k)-(-i+2j) \\\\  & =2i+j+2k \\\\  & =⟨2,1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DE}\\times \\overset{\\to }{DF} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 1)i-(3\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 1)j+(3\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot 2)k \\\\  & =-5i-4j+7k \\\\  & =⟨-5,-4,7⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{DE}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{DF}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{DE}\\times \\overset{\\to }{DF}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-5)^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{7}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{90} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,DEF$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186297487 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM} & =m-l \\\\  & =(3i+2j)-(i-k) \\\\  & =2i+2j+k \\\\  & =⟨2,2,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =n-l \\\\  & =(j+3k)-(i-k) \\\\  & =-i+j+4k \\\\  & =⟨-1,1,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 4-1\\cdot 1)i-(2\\cdot 4-(-1)\\cdot 1)j+(2\\cdot 1-2\\cdot (-1))k \\\\  & =7i-9j+4k \\\\  & =⟨7,-9,4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{7}^{2}+(-9)^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{146}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,LMN$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{146}}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186297489 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{ST}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{SU}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{ST} & =t-s \\\\  & =(2i+3j+k)-(j+2k) \\\\  & =2i+2j-k \\\\  & =⟨2,2,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{SU} & =u-s \\\\  & =(4i+j+2k)-(j+2k) \\\\  & =4i+0j+0k \\\\  & =⟨4,0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{ST}\\times \\overset{\\to }{SU} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot 0)i-(2\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot 4)j+(2\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 4)k \\\\  & =0i-4j-8k \\\\  & =⟨0,-4,-8⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{ST}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{SU}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{ST}\\times \\overset{\\to }{SU}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+(-8)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+64} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{80} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,STU$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{2}=2\\sqrt{5}.\n$$"] [17592186297491 :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $CK$ can be related to the area of $△\\,ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CK\\cdot AB\n$$\n\nSo, to find $CK,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(4i+2j+k)-(i+k) \\\\  & =3i+2j+0k \\\\  & =⟨3,2,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(2i+3j+2k)-(i+k) \\\\  & =i+3j+k \\\\  & =⟨1,3,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 3)i-(3\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 1)j+(3\\cdot 3-2\\cdot 1)k \\\\  & =2i-3j+7k \\\\  & =⟨2,-3,7⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{7}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{62}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{62}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AB$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CK\\cdot AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{62}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}CK\\cdot \\sqrt{13} \\\\ CK & =\\frac{\\sqrt{62}}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ CK & =\\frac{\\sqrt{806}}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186297492 :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $HK$ can be related to the area of $△\\,GHI$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}HK\\cdot GI\n$$\n\nSo, to find $HK,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{GI}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{GH}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{GI} & =i-g \\\\  & =(2i+4j+2k)-(i+j+k) \\\\  & =i+3j+k \\\\  & =⟨1,3,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{GH} & =h-g \\\\  & =(3i+2j+4k)-(i+j+k) \\\\  & =2i+j+3k \\\\  & =⟨2,1,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{GI}\\times \\overset{\\to }{GH} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 1)i-(1\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 2)j+(1\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 2)k \\\\  & =8i-j-5k \\\\  & =⟨8,-1,-5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{GI}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{GH}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{GI}\\times \\overset{\\to }{GH}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{8}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-5)^{2}} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,GHI$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $GI$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}GI & =|\\overset{\\to }{GI}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}HK\\cdot GI,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}HK\\cdot \\sqrt{11} \\\\ HK & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{\\sqrt{11}} \\\\ HK & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{110}}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186297493 :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $TK$ can be related to the area of $△\\,RST$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}TK\\cdot RS\n$$\n\nSo, to find $TK,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{RS}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{RT}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{RS} & =s-r \\\\  & =(7i+4j+5k)-(4i+5j+6k) \\\\  & =3i-j-k \\\\  & =⟨3,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{RT} & =t-r \\\\  & =(6i+8j+4k)-(4i+5j+6k) \\\\  & =2i+3j-2k \\\\  & =⟨2,3,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{RS}\\times \\overset{\\to }{RT} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-1)(-2)-(-1)\\cdot 3)i-(3(-2)-(-1)\\cdot 2)j+(3\\cdot 3-(-1)\\cdot 2)k \\\\  & =5i+4j+11k \\\\  & =⟨5,4,11⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{RS}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{RT}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{RS}\\times \\overset{\\to }{RT}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{11}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{162} \\\\  & =9\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,RST$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $RS$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RS & =|\\overset{\\to }{RS}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}TK\\cdot RS,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}TK\\cdot \\sqrt{11} \\\\ TK & =\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{11}} \\\\ TK & =\\frac{9\\sqrt{22}}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299583 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the scalar triple product $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $a,$ $b,$ and $c.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\\,-\\,(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\\,+\\,3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\,(4\\cdot 5-1\\cdot 1)\\,-\\,(-1)\\,(0\\cdot 5-1\\cdot (-2))\\,+\\,3\\,(0\\cdot 1-4\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =2\\,(19)\\,-\\,(-1)\\,(2)\\,+\\,3\\,(8) \\\\  & =38+2+24 \\\\  & =64.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299584 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the scalar triple product $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $a,$ $b,$ and $c.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\\,-\\,2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\\,+\\,3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\,(0\\cdot 5-1\\cdot (-1))\\,-\\,2\\,(4\\cdot 5-1\\cdot (-2))\\,+\\,3\\,(4\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =1\\,(1)\\,-\\,2\\,(22)\\,+\\,3\\,(-4) \\\\  & =1-44-12 \\\\  & =-55.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299585 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the scalar triple product $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $a,$ $b,$ and $c.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\,-\\,2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\,+\\,5\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\,(1\\cdot (-2)-0\\cdot (-1))\\,-\\,2\\,(3\\cdot (-2)-0\\cdot 4)\\,+\\,5\\,(3\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =0(-2)\\,-\\,2\\,(-6)\\,+\\,5\\,(-7) \\\\  & =0+12-35 \\\\  & =-23.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299586 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the scalar triple product $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $a,$ $b,$ and $c.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 6 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\,-\\,2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\,+\\,3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\,(0\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot 6)\\,-\\,2\\,(4\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot 5)\\,+\\,3\\,(4\\cdot 6-0\\cdot 5) \\\\  & =1\\,(12)\\,-\\,2\\,(14)\\,+\\,3\\,(24) \\\\  & =12-28+72 \\\\  & =56.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299587 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the scalar triple product $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $a,$ $b,$ and $c.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\,-\\,1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\,+\\,3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 6 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\,(4\\cdot 2-0\\cdot (-1))\\,-\\,1\\,(1\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 6)\\,+\\,3\\,(1\\cdot (-1)-4\\cdot 6) \\\\  & =2\\,(8)\\,-\\,1\\,(2)\\,+\\,3\\,(-25) \\\\  & =16-2-75 \\\\  & =-61.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299588 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the scalar triple product $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $a,$ $b,$ and $c.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\\,-\\,0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\\,+\\,2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\,(1\\cdot 5-0\\cdot 2)\\,-\\,0\\,(3\\cdot 5-0\\cdot 4)\\,+\\,2\\,(3\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =1\\,(5)\\,-\\,0\\,(15)\\,+\\,2\\,(2) \\\\  & =5+0+4 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299589 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the scalar triple product $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $a,$ $b,$ and $c.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\,-\\,1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\,+\\,0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\,(3\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 2)\\,-\\,1\\,(0\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 5)\\,+\\,0\\,(0\\cdot 2-3\\cdot 5) \\\\  & =2\\,(-5)\\,-\\,1\\,(-20)\\,+\\,0\\,(-15) \\\\  & =-10+20+0 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299590 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the scalar triple product $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $a,$ $b,$ and $c.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\\,-\\,(-2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\\,+\\,3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)\\,((-1)(-3)-(-2)\\cdot 2)\\,-\\,(-2)\\,(4\\cdot (-3)-(-2)\\cdot 1)\\,+\\,3\\,(4\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =(-1)\\,(7)\\,-\\,(-2)\\,(-10)\\,+\\,3\\,(9) \\\\  & =-7-20+27 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299754 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3\\end{vmatrix} & =2\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{vmatrix}-0\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix}+(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(6-0)-0(-3-0)-1(1-0) \\\\  & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{32}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{32}^{t}={c}_{23}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{23} & =(-1)^{2+3}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(2-0) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{32}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{32}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{23} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{11}\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299756 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & =2\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}-0\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix}+(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(6-0)-0(-2-0)-1(1-0) \\\\  & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{12}^{t}={c}_{21}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{21} & =(-1)^{2+1}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(0+1) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{12}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{12}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{21} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{11}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299757 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & =1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-(-2)\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+0\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(2-(-3))+2(4-0)+0(6-0) \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{33}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{33}^{t}={c}_{33}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{33} & =(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(1+4) \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{33}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{33}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{33} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299758 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =1\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+2\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\,(2+1)-0\\,(0-1)+2\\,(-0-2) \\\\  & =3-0-4 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{31}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{31}^{t}={c}_{13}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{13} & =(-1)^{1+3}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =+\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-0 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{31}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{31}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{13} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1}\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299761 :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\ndet(A)=1-2k\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{12}^{t}={c}_{21}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{21} & =(-1)^{2+1}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(1\\cdot k-0\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =-k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{12}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{12}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{21} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(1-2k)}\\cdot (-k) \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{\\,2k-1\\,}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{12}^{′}=\\frac{1}{3}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{k}{\\,2k-1\\,} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 3k & =2k-1 \\\\ k & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299762 :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\ndet(A)=k-1\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{23}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{23}^{t}={c}_{32}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{32} & =(-1)^{3+2}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(-1+2) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{23}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{23}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{32} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{k-1}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{k-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{23}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{k-1} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ k-1 & =-2 \\\\ k & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299764 :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\ndet(A)=-2k-3\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{23}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{23}^{t}={c}_{32}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{32} & =(-1)^{3+2}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(1+2) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{23}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{23}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{32} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-2k-3)}\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\,2k+3\\,}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{23}^{′}=\\frac{1}{4}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\,2k+3\\,} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 12 & =2k+3 \\\\ 2k & =9 \\\\ k & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299765 :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\ndet(A)=k-2\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{13}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{13}^{t}={c}_{31}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{31} & =(-1)^{3+1}\\begin{vmatrix}k & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}k & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =k+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{13}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{13}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{31} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(k-2)}\\cdot (k+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{k+2}{\\,k-2\\,}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{13}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{k+2}{\\,k-2\\,} & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2(k+2) & =-(k-2) \\\\ 2k+4 & =-k+2 \\\\ 3k & =-2 \\\\ k & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299776 :question/worked-solution "Since we want the entry ${a}_{13}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{31}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{31} & =(-1)^{3+1}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\,1\\cdot (1\\cdot 4-0\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{13}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}\\,{c}_{31} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{17}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299777 :question/worked-solution "Since we want the entry ${a}_{21}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{12}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{12} & =(-1)^{1+2}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\,(1\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{21}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}\\,{c}_{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299778 :question/worked-solution "Since we want the entry ${a}_{33}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{33}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{33} & =(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\,1\\cdot (2\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 0)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{33}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}\\,{c}_{33} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-22}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299801 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ k & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}=1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-2\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ k & 2\\end{vmatrix}+(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ k & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\,(1\\cdot 2-1\\cdot (-1))-2\\,(0\\cdot 2-1\\cdot k)+(-1)\\,(0\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot k) \\\\  & =(2+1)-2\\,(-k)+(-1)\\,(-k) \\\\  & =3+2k+k \\\\  & =3k+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need ${c}_{12}^{t}={c}_{21}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{21} & =(-1)^{2+1}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(2\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =-(4-1) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{12}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{12}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{21} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3k+3}\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{3k+3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we are told that ${a}_{12}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{2}$, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-3}{3k+3} & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -3k-3 & =-6 \\\\ -3k & =-6+3 \\\\ k & =\\frac{-3}{-3} \\\\ k & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299809 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & k & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ k & -2\\end{vmatrix}-(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}+1\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & k\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(1\\cdot (-2)-(-1)\\cdot k)+(0\\cdot (-2)-(-1)\\cdot 3)+(0\\cdot k-1\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =2(k-2)+3-3 \\\\  & =2k-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{23}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{23}^{t}={c}_{32}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{32} & =(-1)^{3+2}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(2\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =-(-2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{23}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{c}_{23}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{c}_{32} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2k-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{k-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{23}^{′}=-1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{k-2} & =-1 \\\\ 1 & =-(k-2) \\\\ k-2 & =-1 \\\\ k & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186299812 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & -2 \\\\ k & -2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2)\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}-(-2)\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ k & -1\\end{vmatrix}+(-2)\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ k & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2)((-1)(-1)-(-2)(-2))+2((-2)(-1)-(-2)k)+(-2)((-2)(-2)-(-1)k) \\\\  & =(-2)(1-4)+2(2+2k)+(-2)(4+k) \\\\  & =6+4+4k-8-2k \\\\  & =2k+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{32}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{32}^{t}={c}_{23}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{23} & =(-1)^{2+3}\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ k & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-((-2)(-2)-(-2)k) \\\\  & =-(4+2k) \\\\  & =-2k-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{32}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{c}_{32}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{c}_{23} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2k-4}{2k+2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{k+2}{k+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{32}^{′}=-2$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{k+2}{k+1} & =-2 \\\\ \\frac{k+2}{k+1} & =2 \\\\ k+2 & =2k+2 \\\\ k & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186300080 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}7 & -3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 8 & -2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -9 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186300308 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 8 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 2 & -9 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186301037 :answer/value "-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 8 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 2 & -9 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186301098 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 8 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 2 & -9 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 3 & 10\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186301122 :answer/value "-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 8 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 2 & -9 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 3 & 10\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186302198 :question/worked-solution "The cross-product rule states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}c\\times f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=c\\times {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nApplying the cross-product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}c\\times f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =c\\times {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(i+j-k)\\times (4\\,i+2\\,k) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(2-0)i-(2+4)j+(0-4)k \\\\  & =2i-6j-4k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186302331 :question/worked-solution "We use the cofactor expansion along the first row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(S) & ={s}_{11}{C}_{11}+{s}_{12}{C}_{12}+{s}_{13}{C}_{13}+{s}_{14}{C}_{14} \\\\  & =0\\cdot {C}_{11}+2\\cdot {C}_{12}+0\\cdot {C}_{13}+0\\cdot {C}_{14} \\\\  & =2{C}_{12} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}∗ & 2 & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ 1 & ∗ & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & ∗ & -5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & ∗ & 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(-1)^{1+2}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2(1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-5 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -5 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{vmatrix}) \\\\  & =-2((-5\\cdot 2-4\\cdot 2)-0+0) \\\\  & =-2\\cdot (-18) \\\\  & =36\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186302372 :question/worked-solution "We use the cofactor expansion along the first row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & ={t}_{11}{C}_{11}+{t}_{12}{C}_{12}+{t}_{13}{C}_{13}+{t}_{14}{C}_{14} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot {C}_{11}+0\\cdot {C}_{12}+4\\cdot {C}_{13}+0\\cdot {C}_{14} \\\\  & =-{C}_{11}+4{C}_{13} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & ∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 6 & 0 & 2 \\\\ ∗ & 0 & 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}+4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}∗ & ∗ & 4 & ∗ \\\\ 9 & 0 & ∗ & 0 \\\\ -1 & 6 & ∗ & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & ∗ & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}+4\\cdot (-1)^{1+3}\\begin{vmatrix}9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}-0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}6 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix}+0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix})+4(9\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}6 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix}-0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}+0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}) \\\\  & =-0+4\\cdot 9(6\\cdot (-2)-2\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =36\\cdot (-12) \\\\  & =-432\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186308559 :question/worked-solution "Suppose the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of these functions is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W\\ne 0$ for at least one $x\\in I,$ then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W=0$ and it’s known that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{x}^{2}+1 & 2+2{x}^{2} \\\\ ({x}^{2}+1)^{′} & (2+2{x}^{2})^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{x}^{2}+1 & 2+2{x}^{2} \\\\ 2x & 4x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+1)(4x)-(2+2{x}^{2})(2x) \\\\  & =4x({x}^{2}+1)-4x({x}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{zero for all }x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set $\\{{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)\\}$ is linearly $\\text{dependent}$ on $R.$"] [17592186308561 :question/worked-solution "Suppose the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of these functions is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W\\ne 0$ for at least one $x\\in I,$ then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W=0$ and it’s known that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}x+1 & {x}^{2} \\\\ (x+1)^{′} & ({x}^{2})^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}x+1 & {x}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 2x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(x+1)\\cdot (2x)-{x}^{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2x(x+1)-{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+2x-{x}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+2x \\\\  & =x(x+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{nonzero for at least one }x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set $\\{{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)\\}$ is linearly $\\text{independent}$ on $R.$"] [17592186308562 :question/worked-solution "Suppose the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of these functions is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W\\ne 0$ for at least one $x\\in I,$ then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W=0$ and it’s known that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}x & {x}^{3}+1 \\\\ (x)^{′} & ({x}^{3}+1)^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}x & {x}^{3}+1 \\\\ 1 & 3{x}^{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =x\\cdot (3{x}^{2})-({x}^{3}+1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3{x}^{3}-{x}^{3}-1 \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{nonzero for at least one }x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly $\\text{independent}$ on $R.$"] [17592186308563 :question/worked-solution "Suppose the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of these functions is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W\\ne 0$ for at least one $x\\in I,$ then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W=0$ and it’s known that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{x} & {e}^{2x} \\\\ ({e}^{x})^{′} & ({e}^{2x})^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{x} & {e}^{2x} \\\\ {e}^{x} & 2{e}^{2x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\cdot 2{e}^{2x}-{e}^{2x}\\cdot {e}^{x} \\\\  & =2{e}^{3x}-{e}^{3x} \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{nonzero for at least one }x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly $\\text{independent}$ on $R.$"] [17592186308587 :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}$ is a solution to the equation” is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =3{e}^{3x} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =9{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{e}^{3x}-6\\cdot 3{e}^{3x}+9{e}^{3x} & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{3x-1}$ is a solution to the equation” is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =3{e}^{3x-1} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =9{e}^{3x-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{e}^{3x-1}-6\\cdot 3{e}^{3x-1}+9{e}^{3x-1} & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation” is false. Both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{3x} & {e}^{3x-1} \\\\ 3{e}^{3x} & 3{e}^{3x-1}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}\\cdot 3{e}^{3x-1}-3{e}^{3x}\\cdot {e}^{3x-1} \\\\  & =3{e}^{6x-1}-3{e}^{6x-1} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian equals $0$ for all $x\\in R,$ the functions are not linearly independent. Hence, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}$ is a solution to the equation”; “${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{3x-1}$ is a solution to the equation” only."] [17592186308589 :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}$ is a solution to the equation” is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =2x \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot 2-3x\\cdot 2x+4{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-6{x}^{2}+4{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)$ is a solution to the equation for $x>0.$” is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+3)-3x(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x)+4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+3{x}^{2}-6{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)-3{x}^{2}+4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0,\\infty ).$” is true. Both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{x}^{2} & {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) \\\\ 2x & 2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x)-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+{x}^{3}-2{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian is not identically $0$ on $(0,\\infty ),$ the functions are linearly independent. Hence, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}$ is a solution to the equation”; “${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)$ is a solution to the equation for $x>0.$”; “The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0,\\infty ).$”."] [17592186308590 :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ is a solution to the equation” is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{2}(x)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ is a solution to the equation” is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation” is false. Both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) & -8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\cdot (-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian equals $0$ for all $x\\in R,$ the functions are not linearly independent. Hence, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ is a solution to the equation”; “${y}_{2}(x)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ is a solution to the equation” only."] [17592186311858 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′} &=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is $f(x)=14x{e}^{-4x}.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-4x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{-4x})({e}^{3x})^{′}-({e}^{-4x})^{′}({e}^{3x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{-4x})(3{e}^{3x})-(-4{e}^{-4x})({e}^{3x}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{-x}+4{e}^{-x} \\\\  & =7{e}^{-x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent. \n\nLet's compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{14x{e}^{-4x}\\cdot {e}^{3x}}{7{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{14x{e}^{-4x}\\cdot {e}^{-4x}}{7{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int x{e}^{-7x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{49}(7x+1){e}^{-7x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(-{x}^{2})({e}^{-4x})+[-\\frac{2}{49}(7x+1){e}^{-7x}]({e}^{3x}) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}{e}^{-4x}-\\frac{2}{49}(7x+1){e}^{-4x} \\\\  & =(-{x}^{2}-\\frac{2x}{7}-\\frac{2}{49}){e}^{-4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{3x}+(-{x}^{2}-\\frac{2x}{7}-\\frac{2}{49}){e}^{-4x} \\\\  & =(A-\\frac{2}{49}){e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{3x}-({x}^{2}+\\frac{2x}{7}){e}^{-4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we redefine the arbitrary constant $A$ as\n\n$$\nA:=(A-\\frac{2}{49})\n$$\n\nour general solution becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{3x}\\underbrace{-({x}^{2}+\\frac{2x}{7}){e}^{-4x}}_{g(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=-({x}^{2}+\\frac{2x}{7}){e}^{-4x}.\n$$"] [17592186311859 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′} &=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is $f(x)=2x{e}^{2x}.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{2x})({e}^{3x})^{′}-({e}^{2x})^{′}({e}^{3x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{2x})(3{e}^{3x})-(2{e}^{2x})({e}^{3x}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{5x}-2{e}^{5x} \\\\  & ={e}^{5x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent. \n\nLet's compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{2x{e}^{2x}\\cdot {e}^{3x}}{{e}^{5x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{2x{e}^{2x}\\cdot {e}^{2x}}{{e}^{5x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int x{e}^{-x}\\text{ }\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2(x+1){e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(-{x}^{2})({e}^{2x})+[-2(x+1){e}^{-x}({e}^{3x})] \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}-2(x+1){e}^{2x} \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+2x+2){e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{3x}-({x}^{2}+2x+2){e}^{2x} \\\\  & =(A-2){e}^{2x}+B{e}^{3x}-({x}^{2}+2x){e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we redefine the arbitrary constant $A$ as\n\n$$\nA:=(A-2)\n$$\n\nour general solution becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{3x}\\,\\underbrace{-\\,({x}^{2}+2x){e}^{2x}}_{g(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=-({x}^{2}+2x){e}^{2x}.\n$$"] [17592186311860 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′} &=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{2x}+Bx{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{2x})(x{e}^{2x})^{′}-({e}^{2x})^{′}(x{e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{2x})({e}^{2x}+2x{e}^{2x})-(2{e}^{2x})(x{e}^{2x}) \\\\  & ={e}^{4x}+2x{e}^{4x}-2x{e}^{4x} \\\\  & ={e}^{4x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent. \n\nLet's compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{{e}^{2x}\\cdot x{e}^{2x}}{{x}^{2}{e}^{4x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x| \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{{e}^{2x}\\cdot {e}^{2x}}{{x}^{2}{e}^{4x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|)({e}^{2x})+\\left(-\\frac{1}{x}\\right)(x{e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =-{e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{e}^{2x}+Bx{e}^{2x}-{e}^{2x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+1) \\\\  & =(A-1){e}^{2x}+Bx{e}^{2x}-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we redefine the arbitrary constant $A$ as\n\n$$\nA:=(A-1)\n$$\n\nour general solution becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{e}^{2x}+Bx{e}^{2x}\\underbrace{\\,-\\,{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|}_{g(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|.\n$$"] [17592186311861 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′} &=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{-x})(x{e}^{-x})^{′}-({e}^{-x})^{′}(x{e}^{-x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{-x})({e}^{-x}-x{e}^{-x})-(-{e}^{-x})(x{e}^{-x}) \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}-x{e}^{-2x}+x{e}^{-2x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent.\n\nLet's compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{{e}^{-x}\\cdot x{e}^{-x}}{{x}^{3}{e}^{-2x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{{e}^{-x}\\cdot {e}^{-x}}{{x}^{3}{e}^{-2x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right){e}^{-x}+\\left(-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}\\right)x{e}^{-x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{x}-\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}+\\underbrace{\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2x}}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{2x}.\n$$"] [17592186311918 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′} &=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx,\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is $f(x)=4{e}^{x}\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x){e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are linearly independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}(x){y}_{2}^{′}(x)-{y}_{1}^{′}(x){y}_{2}(x) \\\\  & =({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)^{′}-({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)^{′}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) \\\\  & =({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)-({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+2{e}^{2x}{cos}^{2}⁡2x-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+2{e}^{2x}{sin}^{2}⁡2x \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x}({cos}^{2}⁡2x+{sin}^{2}⁡2x) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent.\n\nLet’s compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{4{e}^{x}\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x\\cdot {e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{2{e}^{2x}}\\,dx \\\\  & =-2\\int dx \\\\  & =-2x \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{4{e}^{x}\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x\\cdot {e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{2{e}^{2x}}\\,dx \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(-2x)({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|)({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x){e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x){e}^{x}+\\underbrace{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x){e}^{x}}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x){e}^{x}.\n$$"] [17592186311934 :question/worked-solution "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x)={x}^{2}.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{3x})({e}^{-3x})^{′}-({e}^{3x})^{′}({e}^{-3x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{3x})(-3{e}^{-3x})-(3{e}^{3x})({e}^{-3x}) \\\\  & =-3-3 \\\\  & =-6\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{({e}^{3x})}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{({e}^{-3x})}^{′} &={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-3x} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}-3{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-3x} &={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-3x} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for ${u}_{1}.$ We start by eliminating ${u}_{2}^{′}$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}^{′}=-\\frac{{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}}{{e}^{-3x}}=-{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{6x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}-3{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-3x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ 3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}-3(-{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{6x}){e}^{-3x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ 3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}+3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ 6{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{2}{e}^{-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{1}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{6}{x}^{2}{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{1} & =\\int \\frac{1}{6}{x}^{2}{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{1} & =\\int \\frac{1}{6}{x}^{2}{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x)=\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{2}{e}^{-3x}.$"] [17592186311937 :question/worked-solution "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x)=-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{4x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{4x})({e}^{-4x})^{′}-({e}^{4x})^{′}({e}^{-4x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{4x})(-4{e}^{-4x})-(4{e}^{4x})({e}^{-4x}) \\\\  & =-4-4 \\\\  & =-8\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{({e}^{4x})}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{({e}^{-4x})}^{′} &=-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{4x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-4x} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{4x}-4{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-4x} &=-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{4x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-4x} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for ${u}_{1}.$ We start by eliminating ${u}_{2}^{′}$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}^{′}=-\\frac{{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{4x}}{{e}^{-4x}}=-{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{8x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{4x}-4{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-4x} & =-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ 4{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{4x}-4(-{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{8x}){e}^{-4x} & =-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ 4{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{4x}+4{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{4x} & =-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ 8{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{4x} & =-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′} & =-2{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{1}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -2{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{1} & =-\\int 2{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x)=2{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$"] [17592186311938 :question/worked-solution "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x)={x}^{2}.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{-x})({e}^{-2x})^{′}-({e}^{-x})^{′}({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{-x})(-2{e}^{-2x})-(-{e}^{-x})({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-3x}+{e}^{-3x} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-3x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{({e}^{-x})}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{({e}^{-2x})}^{′} &={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\,{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} &={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for ${u}_{1}.$ We start by eliminating ${u}_{2}^{′}$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}^{′}=-\\frac{{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{-2x}}=-{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\,{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ -\\,{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}-2(-{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}){e}^{-2x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ -\\,{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}+2{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′} & ={x}^{2}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{1}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{1} & =\\int {x}^{2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x)={x}^{2}{e}^{x}.$"] [17592186311939 :question/worked-solution "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x)={x}^{2}.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{-x})({e}^{-2x})^{′}-({e}^{-x})^{′}({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{-x})(-2{e}^{-2x})-(-{e}^{-x})({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-3x}+{e}^{-3x} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-3x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{({e}^{-x})}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{({e}^{-2x})}^{′} &={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\,{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} &={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for ${u}_{2}.$ We start by eliminating ${u}_{1}^{′}$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}^{′}=-\\frac{{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}}{{e}^{-x}}=-{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\,{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ -\\,(-{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-x}){e}^{-x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ -\\,{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{2}^{′} & =-{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{2}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{2} & =-\\int {x}^{2}{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x)={x}^{2}{e}^{2x}.$"] [17592186311945 :question/worked-solution "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x)=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{3x})({e}^{-2x})^{′}-({e}^{3x})^{′}({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{3x})(-2{e}^{-2x})-(3{e}^{3x})({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{x}-3{e}^{x} \\\\  & =-5{e}^{x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{({e}^{3x})}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{({e}^{-2x})}^{′} &=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} &=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for ${u}_{2}.$ We start by eliminating ${u}_{1}^{′}$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}^{′}=-\\frac{{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}}{{e}^{3x}}=-{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-5x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ 3(-{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-5x}){e}^{3x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ -3{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ -5{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{2}^{′} & =-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{2}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{2} & =-\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x)={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$"] [17592186311946 :question/worked-solution "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x)=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{3x})({e}^{-2x})^{′}-({e}^{3x})^{′}({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{3x})(-2{e}^{-2x})-(3{e}^{3x})({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{x}-3{e}^{x} \\\\  & =-5{e}^{x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{({e}^{3x})}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{({e}^{-2x})}^{′} &=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} &=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for ${u}_{1}.$ We start by eliminating ${u}_{2}^{′}$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}^{′}=-\\frac{{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}}{{e}^{-2x}}=-{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ 3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}-2(-{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{5x}){e}^{-2x} & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ 3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}+2{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x} & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ 5{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x} & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′} & ={e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{1}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{1} & =\\int {e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x)={e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$"] [17592186314698 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′} &=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx,\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is $f(x)={csc}^{3}⁡2x.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}(x){y}_{2}^{′}(x)-{y}_{1}^{′}(x){y}_{2}(x) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)^{′}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)-(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡2x+2{sin}^{2}⁡2x \\\\  & =2\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent. \n\nLet's compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x)$:\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{{csc}^{3}⁡2x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{2}\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {csc}^{2}⁡2x\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cot}⁡2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{{csc}^{3}⁡2x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{2}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{{sin}^{3}⁡2x}\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.$ Then $du=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,dx,$ so $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,dx=\\frac{1}{2}\\,du,$ and the integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{{sin}^{3}⁡2x}\\,dx & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}(\\frac{1}{2}\\,du) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int {u}^{-3}\\,du \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}{u}^{-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8{sin}^{2}⁡2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cot}⁡2x)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)+\\left(-\\frac{1}{8{sin}^{2}⁡2x}\\right)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡2x}{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}-\\frac{1}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}(2{cos}^{2}⁡2x-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,+\\,\\underbrace{\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x.\n$$"] [17592186314734 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′} &=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx,\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is $f(x)=x{e}^{2x}.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)={e}^{2x}\\,(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are linearly independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}(x){y}_{2}^{′}(x)-{y}_{1}^{′}(x){y}_{2}(x) \\\\  & =({e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)({e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}-({e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{′}({e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =({e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-(2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)({e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & ={e}^{4x}({cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & ={e}^{4x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent.\n\nLet’s compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{x{e}^{2x}\\cdot {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{e}^{4x}}\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{x{e}^{2x}\\cdot {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{e}^{4x}}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)({e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)({e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\,(x{cos}^{2}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+x{sin}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =x{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\,(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+\\underbrace{x{e}^{2x}}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=x{e}^{2x}.\n$$"] [17592186314735 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′} &=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx,\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is $f(x)={e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)={e}^{-x}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are linearly independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}(x){y}_{2}^{′}(x)-{y}_{1}^{′}(x){y}_{2}(x) \\\\  & =({e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)({e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}-({e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{′}({e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =({e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(-{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-(-{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)({e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}({cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent.\n\nLet’s compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\cdot {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{e}^{-2x}}\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\int \\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x| \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\cdot {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{e}^{-2x}}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\int dx \\\\  & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|)({e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(x{e}^{-x}) \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+\\underbrace{{e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)={e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\n$$"] [17592186314743 :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ and $y(1)+2{y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\ne 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-16\\lambda m+64{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-8\\lambda )(m-8\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m=8\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{8\\lambda x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{8\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{8\\lambda x}+Bx{e}^{8\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =8A\\lambda {e}^{8\\lambda x}+B{e}^{8\\lambda x}+8B\\lambda x{e}^{8\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8A\\lambda +B & =0 \\\\ (8\\lambda )A+B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(1)+2{y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{8\\lambda }+B{e}^{8\\lambda }+2(8A\\lambda {e}^{8\\lambda }+B{e}^{8\\lambda }+8B\\lambda {e}^{8\\lambda }) & =0 \\\\ A+B+2(8A\\lambda +B+8B\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ A+B+16A\\lambda +2B+16B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (1+16\\lambda )A+(3+16\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following systems of equations for $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(8\\lambda )A+B &=0 \\\\ (1+16\\lambda )A+(3+16\\lambda )B &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis homogeneous linear system has a nontrivial solution if and only if the determinant is zero. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}8\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 1+16\\lambda  & 3+16\\lambda \\end{vmatrix}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\lambda (3+16\\lambda )-(1)(1+16\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ 24\\lambda +128{\\lambda }^{2}-1-16\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ 128{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda -1 & =0 \\\\ (16\\lambda -1)(8\\lambda +1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have the eigenvalues $$\n\\displaystyle {\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{8}\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{16}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the eigenfunction corresponding to each eigenvalue:\n\n- Consider ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{8}.$ Then, we have the solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & =A{e}^{8{\\lambda }_{1}x}+Bx{e}^{8{\\lambda }_{1}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{1}$ into\n\n$$\n(8\\lambda )A+B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n-A+B=0\\,⇒\\,A=B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=B{e}^{-x}(x+1).\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{16},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =A{e}^{8{\\lambda }_{2}x}+Bx{e}^{8{\\lambda }_{2}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{2}$ into\n\n$$\n(8\\lambda )A+B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}A+B=0\\,⇒\\,A=-2B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =-2B{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2} \\\\  & =B{e}^{x/2}(x-2) \\\\  & =C{e}^{x/2}(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have set $C=B.$\n\nSo, the eigenfunctions corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively, are $B{e}^{-x}(x+1)\\text{ and }C{e}^{x/2}(x-2).$"] [17592186314744 :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ and $y(1)+2{y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\ne 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-12\\lambda m+36{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-6\\lambda )(m-6\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m=6\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{6\\lambda x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{6\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{6\\lambda x}+Bx{e}^{6\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =6A\\lambda {e}^{6\\lambda x}+B{e}^{6\\lambda x}+6B\\lambda x{e}^{6\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6A\\lambda +B & =0 \\\\ (6\\lambda )A+B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(1)+2{y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{6\\lambda }+B{e}^{6\\lambda }+2(6A\\lambda {e}^{6\\lambda }+B{e}^{6\\lambda }+6B\\lambda {e}^{6\\lambda }) & =0 \\\\ A+B+2(6A\\lambda +B+6B\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ A+B+12A\\lambda +2B+12B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (1+12\\lambda )A+(3+12\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following systems of equations for $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(6\\lambda )A+B &=0 \\\\ (1+12\\lambda )A+(3+12\\lambda )B &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis homogeneous linear system has a nontrivial solution if and only if the determinant is zero. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}6\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 1+12\\lambda  & 3+12\\lambda \\end{vmatrix}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\lambda (3+12\\lambda )-(1)(1+12\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ 18\\lambda +72{\\lambda }^{2}-1-12\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ 72{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda -1 & =0 \\\\ (12\\lambda -1)(6\\lambda +1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have the eigenvalues $$\n\\displaystyle {\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{6}\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{12}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the eigenfunction corresponding to each eigenvalue:\n\n- Consider ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{6}.$ Then, we have the solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & =A{e}^{6{\\lambda }_{1}x}+Bx{e}^{6{\\lambda }_{1}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{1}$ into\n\n$$\n(6\\lambda )A+B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n-A+B=0\\,⇒\\,A=B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=B{e}^{-x}(x+1).\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{12},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =A{e}^{6{\\lambda }_{2}x}+Bx{e}^{6{\\lambda }_{2}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{2}$ into\n\n$$\n(6\\lambda )A+B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}A+B=0\\,⇒\\,A=-2B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =-2B{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2} \\\\  & =B{e}^{x/2}(x-2) \\\\  & =C{e}^{x/2}(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have set $C=B.$\n\nSo, the eigenfunctions corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively, are $B{e}^{-x}(x+1)\\text{ and }C{e}^{x/2}(x-2).$"] [17592186314820 :question/worked-solution "From the equation of a plane in the parametric form $r=p+tv+sw$, we have that its normal vector is $n=v\\times w.$ In our case, the normal vectors to $Π$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =v\\times w \\\\  & =⟨1,0,4⟩\\times ⟨2,6,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =-24i+8j+6k \\\\  & =⟨-24,8,6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane $Π$ in dot product form is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-24,8,6⟩ & =⟨-12,10,6⟩\\cdot ⟨-24,8,6⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-24,8,6⟩ & =404.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $r=⟨1,3,2⟩+w⟨-5,-2,-1⟩$ into the equation of the plane in dot product form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot ⟨-24,8,6⟩ & =404 \\\\ ⟨1-5w,3-2w,2-w⟩\\cdot ⟨-24,8,6⟩ & =404 \\\\ -24(1-5w)+8(3-2w)+6(2-w) & =404 \\\\ -24+120w+24-16w+12-6w & =404 \\\\ 98w & =392 \\\\ w & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to get the position vector $q$ of the intersection point $Q,$ we substitute $w=4$ into the vector equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q & =⟨1-5(4),3-2(4),2-(4)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-19,-5,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection point is $Q(-19,-5,-2).$"] [17592186317438 :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})⟩=⟨2t,3{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(2t)^{2}+(3{t}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+9{t}^{4}} \\\\  & =t\\sqrt{4+9{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since $t>0$, we have $|t|=t.$\n\nNow, we find the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2})⟩=⟨2,6t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(t)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t) & =⟨2t,\\,3{t}^{2},\\,0⟩\\times ⟨2,\\,6t,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2t & 3{t}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 2 & 6t & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2t & 3{t}^{2} \\\\ 2 & 6t\\end{vmatrix}\\,k \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the magnitude of the cross product is\n\n$$\n‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖=6{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{t}^{2}}{(t\\sqrt{4+9{t}^{2}})^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{t}^{2}}{{t}^{3}(4+9{t}^{2})^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{t(4+9{t}^{2})^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186317524 :question/worked-solution "According to the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}+{u}_{3}{y}_{3},\\,{u}_{i}=(-1)^{3-i}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{i}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,i=1,2,3,\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is the forcing term, $W(x)$ is the Wronskian of ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{1}(x)=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{2} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{vmatrix},\\,{W}_{2}(x)=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{vmatrix},\\,{W}_{3}(x)=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the forcing term is $g(x)=12{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}={e}^{-2x},\\,{y}_{2}={e}^{x},\\,{y}_{3}={e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(x) & =W({y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{-2x} & {e}^{x} & {e}^{3x} \\\\ -2{e}^{-2x} & {e}^{x} & 3{e}^{3x} \\\\ 4{e}^{-2x} & {e}^{x} & 9{e}^{3x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =({e}^{-2x}\\cdot {e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{3x})\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 9\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}[1(9-3)-1(-18-12)+1(-2-4)] \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}(6+30-6) \\\\  & =30{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, to find ${u}_{2},$ we compute the determinant\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{2}(x) & ={W}_{2}({y}_{1},{y}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{-2x} & {e}^{3x} \\\\ -2{e}^{-2x} & 3{e}^{3x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3{e}^{x}-(-2{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =5{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2}(x) & =(-1)^{3-2}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{2}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{(12{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(5{e}^{x})}{30{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186317525 :question/worked-solution "According to the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}+{u}_{3}{y}_{3},\\,{u}_{i}=(-1)^{3-i}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{i}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,i=1,2,3,\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is the forcing term, $W(x)$ is the Wronskian of ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{1}(x)=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{2} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{vmatrix},\\,{W}_{2}(x)=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{vmatrix},\\,{W}_{3}(x)=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the forcing term is $g(x)=\\frac{6{e}^{3x}}{1+{e}^{2x}}.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}={e}^{-4x},\\,{y}_{2}={e}^{x},\\,{y}_{3}={e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(x) & =W({y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{-4x} & {e}^{x} & {e}^{2x} \\\\ -4{e}^{-4x} & {e}^{x} & 2{e}^{2x} \\\\ 16{e}^{-4x} & {e}^{x} & 4{e}^{2x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =({e}^{-4x}\\cdot {e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{2x})\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 16 & 1 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}[1(4-2)-1(-16-32)+1(-4-16)] \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}(2-(-48)+(-20)) \\\\  & =30{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, to find ${u}_{3},$ we compute the determinant\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{3}(x) & ={W}_{3}({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{-4x} & {e}^{x} \\\\ -4{e}^{-4x} & {e}^{x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3x}-(-4{e}^{-3x}) \\\\  & =5{e}^{-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{3}(x) & =(-1)^{3-3}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{3}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\left(\\frac{6{e}^{3x}}{1+{e}^{2x}}\\right)(5{e}^{-3x})}{30{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{1+{({e}^{x})}^{2}}\\cdot {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({e}^{x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186317526 :question/worked-solution "According to the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}+{u}_{3}{y}_{3},\\,{u}_{i}=(-1)^{3-i}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{i}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,i=1,2,3,\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is the forcing term, $W(x)$ is the Wronskian of ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{1}(x)=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{2} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{vmatrix},\\,{W}_{2}(x)=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{vmatrix},\\,{W}_{3}(x)=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the forcing term is $g(x)=\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}=1,\\,{y}_{2}={e}^{-3x},\\,{y}_{3}={e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(x) & =W({y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & {e}^{-3x} & {e}^{4x} \\\\ 0 & -3{e}^{-3x} & 4{e}^{4x} \\\\ 0 & 9{e}^{-3x} & 16{e}^{4x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(1\\cdot {e}^{-3x}\\cdot {e}^{4x})\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 16\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}[1(-48-36)-1(0-0)+1(0-0)] \\\\  & =-84{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are linearly independent.\n\nNow, to find ${u}_{1},$ we compute the determinant\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{1}(x) & ={W}_{1}({y}_{2},{y}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{2} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{-3x} & {e}^{4x} \\\\ -3{e}^{-3x} & 4{e}^{4x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4{e}^{x}-(-3{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =7{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =(-1)^{3-1}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{1}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\right)(7{e}^{x})}{(-84{e}^{x})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}\\int \\left(\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186317527 :question/worked-solution "According to the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}+{u}_{3}{y}_{3},\\,{u}_{i}=(-1)^{3-i}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{i}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,i=1,2,3,\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is the forcing term, $W(x)$ is the Wronskian of ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{1}(x)=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{2} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{vmatrix},\\,{W}_{2}(x)=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{vmatrix},\\,{W}_{3}(x)=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the forcing term is $g(x)=35{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}={e}^{-5x},\\,{y}_{2}={e}^{-3x},\\,{y}_{3}={e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(x) & =W({y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{-5x} & {e}^{-3x} & {e}^{2x} \\\\ -5{e}^{-5x} & -3{e}^{-3x} & 2{e}^{2x} \\\\ 25{e}^{-5x} & 9{e}^{-3x} & 4{e}^{2x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =({e}^{-5x}\\cdot {e}^{-3x}\\cdot {e}^{2x})\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -5 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 25 & 9 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{-6x}[1(-12-18)-1(-20-50)+1(-45+75)] \\\\  & ={e}^{-6x}(-30-(-70)+30) \\\\  & =70{e}^{-6x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, to find ${u}_{3},$ we compute the determinant\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{3}(x) & ={W}_{3}({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{-5x} & {e}^{-3x} \\\\ -5{e}^{-5x} & -3{e}^{-3x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3{e}^{-8x}-(-5{e}^{-8x}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{-8x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{3}(x) & =(-1)^{3-3}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{3}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{(35{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(2{e}^{-8x})}{70{e}^{-6x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186317548 :question/worked-solution "According to the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}+{u}_{3}{y}_{3},\\,{u}_{i}=(-1)^{3-i}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{i}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,i=1,2,3,\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is the forcing term, $W(x)$ is the Wronskian of ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{1}(x)=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{2} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{vmatrix},\\,{W}_{2}(x)=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{vmatrix},\\,{W}_{3}(x)=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the forcing term is $g(x)=3{e}^{x}.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}=1,\\,{y}_{2}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x),\\,{y}_{3}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(x) & =W({y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) \\\\ 0 & -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) & 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) \\\\ 0 & -9\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & -9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) & 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) \\\\ -9\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & -9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =27{sin}^{2}⁡(3x)-(-27{cos}^{2}⁡(3x)) \\\\  & =27{sin}^{2}⁡(3x)+27{cos}^{2}⁡(3x) \\\\  & =27[{sin}^{2}⁡(3x)+{cos}^{2}⁡(3x)] \\\\  & =27\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, to find ${u}_{1},$ we compute the determinant ${W}_{1}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{1}(x) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{2} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) \\\\ -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) & 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3{cos}^{2}⁡(3x)+3{sin}^{2}⁡(3x) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =(-1)^{3-1}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{1}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{3{e}^{x}\\cdot 3}{27}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]}
